**Setup.** Frozen Table-1 Burgers 2D model (dn256b, R=512, K=16, rotation_R512); three settings: accurate span R'=384/M=1536, fast span R'=128/M=512, head k=16/q=0/M=64; LM gtol 1e-3 everywhere. Only the tested advection is replaced (the user's hybrid): dense upwind on all nodes, mesh rules (deployed 63² lattice `lat64`; head's fitted NNLS EQ `q0scaled`), or Hari's off-mesh form L Σ w_q ψ(x_q) u(u_x+u_y)(x_q) (and flux form) with the bank + forward-mode gradient at Gauss 32²–256², Fibonacci 1597–46368, Sobol 4096; continuum rollout `gref` = Gauss 640² (certified vs 768² and flux form, G6); controls Smolyak CC 8 and Gauss 8². Refined references at 8192²: ST (dt/16, primary) and S (same dt), on the shared 257² nodes. Cohorts dev6, val32 (= sel32), test64 (reused historical cohort; nothing chosen from it).

**Jobs (9).** refdv 4734273 (A100, 76 refs, all accepted), reft 4734276 (A100, 128 refs, all accepted), fz4096 4735747 (H200, feasibility, 1 dev case), dv256 4735696 / dv1024 4735709 (A100-80G), dv4096 4735717 (H200), t256 4739520 / t1024 4739521 (A100-80G), t4096 4739522 (H200). Every applicable gate passes in every ROM job (G1–G8 incl. parity with the Table-1/Table-2 records to ≤1.3e-13, both controls failing everywhere, NumPy audit of errors ≤1e-10 and ρ ≤1e-6, timed outputs identical to phase 1).

**Found (numbers from the generated report).**
- (i) Linear rungs: resolved off-mesh rules give mesh-invariant errors case by case (largest per-case ST spread over 256²/1024²/4096²: 0.020 pp dev, 0.011 pp test; B2 1.0001–1.0060 on dev). Dense (upwind) improves with the mesh. Matched dev cases, accurate: continuum rollout ST 2.75/2.75/1.82 %, S 1.06/1.05/0.24 % vs dense ST 6.21/3.38/1.91 %, S 4.16/1.34/0.24 % (38/38/6 cases); test: 2.64/2.65/1.57 % and 1.34/1.36/0.10 % vs dense 5.58/3.27/1.62 % and 3.71/1.53/0.10 % (64/64/6). So the off-mesh solve on a coarse mesh is more accurate (cohort-worst) than dense there, and at 4096² the worst errors are close (slightly lower vs ST, slightly higher vs S). Head setting: NOT mesh-invariant on test (B2 1.91; cases 36/48/60 move up to 4.7 pp for every arm, dense and lat64 included) — a head-solve effect, cause not isolated.
- (ii) Our bank converges far more slowly in m than Hari's (Gauss 64², Fibonacci ~4–7k: continuum ρ_max ~1e-2 at the accurate setting; Gauss 128² ~2–7e-3; continuum target needed 640²). At ~4k points: lat64 5.4e-2 vs its mesh target, Gauss 64² 1.9e-2 / Fibonacci 4181 3.3e-2 vs the continuum (dev, 1024², accurate) — different targets. Sobol 4096 ~0.1–0.25, Smolyak 8 up to 9.5 (fail). Deployed lat64 fails B3 at accurate 256²/1024² (0.13 / 0.27 % from dense); fitted head EQ fails B3 at 256²/1024² (0.65 / 0.81 %).
- (iii) Solve time (median query − median decode) is flat in N on one H200: B4 ratios 0.937–1.060 (dev) and 0.972–1.040 (test) for all 40 arms; grows with m. Full query is not flat (decode of six output fields grows with N). At 4096²: accurate Gauss 96² 54.5 ms vs lat64 46.3 ms; fast Fibonacci 1597 13.3 vs 18.7 ms; head Gauss 32² 22.3 vs 40.6 ms (not a matched equal-accuracy comparison).
- (iv) Not Hari's narrow-early-bump pattern: for off-mesh Gauss/Fibonacci the largest-ρ cases are the wider bumps (Spearman with width +0.26…+0.64), the single worst state is often k=1 but the top-1 % tail spreads to later steps; lat64 neutral/negative. Cause not tested.
- Selection: pre-registered recommended rule = none in every setting (two-sided B1 fails because resolved off-mesh rules are better than dense at the coarse meshes). Post hoc (one-sided B1′, frozen before the test jobs): accurate Gauss 96², fast Fibonacci 1597, head Gauss 32². On test64: accurate and fast CONFIRMED; head Gauss 32² NOT confirmed (B3 0.585/1.105/1.258 % vs 0.5 %).

