   991	## Amendment A9 (2026-10-18) — anchor round-off floor (CPU dry run evidence, no verdict data)
   992	
   993	Evidence: after A8, on ECSW problems the QR-based native Gauss–Newton at the **tighter** level (γ ≤ 1e-8) stalled at
   994	γ ≈ 2–5e-8 on 2–11 of 50 steps (no residual decrease possible at working precision: exit reason 3), while its tight vs
   995	tighter distance was ≤ 1e-7. The ratio γ has a round-off floor near $\epsilon\,\|c\|/\|\hat r\|$, which is large when the
   996	sampled residual is small (ECSW supports close to r).
   997	
   998	**A9.1 Anchor validity.** An anchor step passes if γ ≤ gtol(level), **or** it is a round-off stall — the native solver
   999	exhausted its backtracking (exit reason 3) — with γ ≤ 100 × gtol(level). Round-off stalls are counted and reported per
  1000	configuration. Everything else in A2.7 stands (finite trajectory, no damping saturation, every step accounted for), and
  1001	the binding converged-anchor check remains the tight/tighter agreement ≤ 2.5e-5 on the prescribed subset. This
  1002	changes only the lane's own anchor-validity rule (A1.7/A2.7), not the COMMON-BRIEF Q1 predicates for the arm's runs
  1003	(where exit reason 3 still makes a run invalid).
  1004	
  1005	**A10.1 (2026-10-18, dry-run evidence) N-time fixture.** N-time times the `none` arm at the smoke rank (BE, native):
  1006	its calls are long enough for stable timing, whereas the ECSW calls in the dry run lasted a few milliseconds and gave
  1007	T = 1.79 on one CPU cell. Ours' A–B–A pairs with the ECSW fixture as before. The band [1.8, 2.2] is unchanged.
